1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
When you consume model output in code, you hit a problem the happy path hides.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A generation can come back malformed, truncated, or missing a field,

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
and naive parsing crashes the whole pipeline on it.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This lesson covers how to read output defensively so one bad response fails safely. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Let's start with what defensive parsing actually means, and it comes down to three habits.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, tolerant extraction.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You pull the fields you need without assuming the entire payload is well formed.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
You read what's there and cope when something isn't.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Second, explicit failure handling.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
You decide in advance what happens when output is bad, a wrapped parse, a fallback, a reprompt,

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
so there's always a defined path, and never an unhandled crash.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, no blind trust.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
You treat every field as possibly missing or the wrong type.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Put those together, and a single bad generation degrades gracefully

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
instead of taking your pipeline down with it.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Tolerant, explicit, distrustful.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
That's the mindset for the rest of this lesson.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's the first concrete habit, and it trips up almost everyone.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
A response can parse cleanly as JSON and still not have the shape your code expects.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Valid JSON isn't the same as valid data,

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
so confirm each required field actually exists before you read it.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Check the type before you do math on a number or string work on a string,

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
because the model might hand you the wrong one.

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And treat an empty value or a null as a real expected case, not an impossible edge.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The payoff is where the failure lands.

26
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
When you guard every access, a surprising payload fails right at the check you wrote

27
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
with a clear message.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Skip the guards, and it blows up three functions deep, where the cause is a mystery.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Let me frame the choice directly.

30
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Trusting output versus parsing it.

31
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
When you trust it, you read fields straight off the response and act on them immediately.

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
That works right up until one malformed or missing value throws deep inside your logic,

33
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
and now a single bad generation has crashed the entire flow.

34
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
When you parse it, you extract through checks that expect surprises.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
A bad value gets caught at the boundary, logged and handled,

36
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
so one odd response degrades quietly instead of taking everything down.

37
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Notice this isn't about distrusting the model's intelligence.

38
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Even a perfectly capable model is non-deterministic, so the same prompt can vary run to run.

39
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Parsing defensively is just how you make a probabilistic output source safe to build on.

40
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Last lesson gave you the roster of ways a structured response can go sideways,

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:59,000
refusal, truncation, enum case, property order, and the rule to read stop reason before you parse.

42
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Now let's put that roster to work on a real debugging scenario.

43
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Picture a ticket triage extractor.

44
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
It pulls a priority and a summary out of every incoming ticket, and it has run clean for days.

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Then one overnight batch crashes with a key error on priority.

46
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Three functions deep in the pipeline.

47
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
The first instinct is to stare at the crash site, but the crash site isn't the cause.

48
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The stack trace just tells you where the damage finally surfaced.

49
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The real question is what the model actually returned, so that's where the diagnosis starts.

50
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Walk the diagnosis in order.

51
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Step one, read stop reason on the failing response, and there's the first clue.

52
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It says max tokens, not in turn.

53
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Generation hit the ceiling, which means the model never finished writing the object.

54
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Step two, inspect the raw payload, and the story fills in.

55
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
One unusually long ticket ate most of the token budget,

56
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and the JSON ends mid-object with the closing braces missing.

57
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
No parser on earth finishes that cleanly.

58
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Step three, name the missing guard.

59
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
The code went straight to parsing and field access without ever asking why the model stopped.

60
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
A known documented edge case landed in production as a mystery crash,

61
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
purely because the check that would have named it wasn't there.

62
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Now let's write the hardened version, and notice every fix lands at the boundary

63
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
before any field is read.

64
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
First, branch on stop reason.

65
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
A refusal routes to the refusal path.

66
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
A max token stop routes to a retry with a bigger budget,

67
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
or a truncation handler, and only in turn earns the parse.

68
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Second, guard the access itself.

69
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Read each field by name, confirm it exists, and check the type before using it.

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Third, when you compare enum values, compare them case insensitively.

71
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Exactly as the roster from last lesson says.

72
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Rerun the overnight batch with those guards in place,

73
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and the same bad generation produces one clear log line about a truncated response

74
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
instead of a crash three functions deep.

75
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
That's the whole payoff of defensive parsing.

76
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The failure still happens, but it fails at the check with a name.

77
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
OK, so a parse fails, or validation rejects the output.

78
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
What do you actually do?

79
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
You have three safe responses, and picking one deliberately is the whole point.

80
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
First, fail loud.

81
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
When output can't be parsed, raise a clear error right at the boundary

82
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
instead of passing garbage downstream to blow up somewhere confusing later.

83
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Second, fall back.

84
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Have a default path ready, a safe value, a cached result, a degraded mode,

85
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
so one bad generation doesn't stall the user entirely.

86
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Third, re-prompt.

87
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Send the response back to the model with the error described,

88
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
and give it a chance to correct what it got wrong.

89
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Fail loud, fall back, or re-prompt.

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
What you never do is let unreadable output pass through as if it were fine.

91
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
One failure mode is worth flagging before we close,

92
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
because none of today's guards will catch it.

93
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
A silent failure returns success while the content is actually wrong,

94
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and since it parses cleanly, every check we built in this lesson waves it through.

95
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Picture the triage extractor again,

96
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
only this time it labels an outage ticket as low priority.

97
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Every field is present, the types check out, stop, reason says in turn,

98
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
and the pipeline runs on happily while an urgent ticket sits at the bottom of the queue.

99
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Catching that takes a semantic gate, a check aimed at meaning instead of shape,

100
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
and that failure is important enough that the next lesson makes it the whole subject.

101
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Today's guards prove the output is well formed.

102
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
They don't prove it's right. Let's recap.

103
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Defensive parsing keeps one bad generation from crashing your entire pipeline.

104
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Extract tolerantly, check the stop reason before you parse,

105
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
guard even structured output, and put every check at the boundary where a failure gets a name.

106
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Just remember, valid JSON is not necessarily valid data.

107
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
That covers parsing model output defensively.

108
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
I'll see you in the next one.

